    "q-149404": {
      "dom_id": "step-q149404",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1,$ $4,$ and $8.$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-149404-a",
              "type": "math",
